A||||4||Shiner||No AC belt: Problem?||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||03-15-2000||03:55 AM||||When I bought my '87 GT, there was no belt connected to the ac compressor.  I figured that the bearings froze up or something, and the previous owner just took off the belt.  Anyway, would this have any negative side effects?  (besides the AC not working, lol)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Luperman||03-15-2000||05:01 AM||||none that comes to mind,should not affect anything.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Carrolles||03-15-2000||12:40 PM||||I agree with Luperman.  There is nothing else connected to that belt.  You can still turn the A/C switch to the on position to make the cooling fans come on.<P>It could be that the belt just wore out and the previous owner didn't want to mess with replacing it.  With the engine off, ignition on and A/C switch on try to turn the A/C compressor by hand to see if it is locked up.<P>When I got my car it also did not have an A/C belt.  I finally got around to installing one and it worked great for 1-1/2 years.<P>You never know.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-15-2000||12:41 PM||||I agree with Luperman.  There is nothing else connected to that belt.  You can still turn the A/C switch to the on position to make the cooling fans come on.<P>It could be that the belt just worn out and the previous owner didn't want to mess with replacing it.  With the engine off, ignition on and A/C switch on try to turn the A/C compressor by hand to see if it is locked up.<P>When I got my car it also did not have an A/C belt.  I finally got around to installing one and it worked great for 1-1/2 years.<P>You never know.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-15-2000||07:03 PM||||put a wrench on the AC shaft nut and turn it in the same direction as normal. You likely won't be able to turn it by hand otherwise, it's pretty stiff.||||reg||1
